1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,660
The alley.

2
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:01,420
The stranger.

3
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:02,340
The dog.

4
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,780
And you, standing there in your sunglasses, trying

5
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:06,300
very hard not to panic.

6
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:08,540
Don't think about the toothbrush, you tell yourself.

7
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:09,700
Don't think about the toothbrush.

8
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,340
The stranger tilts their head.

9
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:12,320
Toothbrush?

10
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:13,480
Your stomach drops.

11
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:14,780
They can hear you.

12
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:15,840
Every thought.

13
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:17,440
Relax, they say.

14
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:18,660
I'm not here to hurt you.

15
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:20,460
I just wanted to see if you'd follow

16
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:20,740
me.

17
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:21,460
You did.

18
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:23,540
So what if you could read minds?

19
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:24,340
Not guess.

20
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:26,120
Actually hear what people think.

21
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:28,920
Here's what you'd be listening to.

22
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:32,400
Your brain has about eighty-six billion nerve cells,

23
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:33,140
called neurons.

24
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,020
Every thought you have is millions of them

25
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:38,000
firing tiny electrical pulses.

26
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:39,240
Sounds loud.

27
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:40,380
It isn't.

28
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:42,580
By the time those signals reach the outside

29
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:45,440
of your skull, they're about fifty millionths of

30
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:45,720
a volt.

31
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:48,420
The magnetic field your brain makes is weaker

32
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:48,820
still.

33
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:50,960
One researcher puts it at more than a

34
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,080
billion times weaker than the Earth's.

35
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:56,520
To measure it, scientists use super-cold sensors inside

36
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,360
rooms shielded from the outside world.

37
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:00,520
A passing car can drown it out.

38
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:02,940
So hearing thoughts from across a street?

39
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:05,140
With known physics, that's impossible.

40
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:07,500
But the stranger can.

41
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:09,220
And they tell you what it's like.

42
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:11,200
Imagine a crowded market, they say.

43
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:14,220
Now imagine hearing every single person, all at

44
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:14,580
once.

45
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:15,960
Did I leave the oven on.

46
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:16,900
I hate this song.

47
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:18,280
Is that dog looking at me.

48
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:19,520
It never stops.

49
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:21,380
That's why I like alleys.

50
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:24,060
And here's the strange part.

51
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:26,680
Scientists are starting to do a tiny version

52
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:27,100
of it.

53
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:29,500
In 2023, a team at the University of

54
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:31,960
Texas put people inside a brain scanner while

55
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:33,100
they listened to podcasts.

56
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:35,360
For about sixteen hours each.

57
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:37,840
A computer learned which brain patterns went with

58
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:38,460
which ideas.

59
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:41,520
Then, when people imagined telling a story, it

60
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:43,420
turned their brain activity into text.

61
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,060
Not word for word.

62
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:46,180
It got the gist.

63
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,120
But it only worked on the person it

64
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:48,700
was trained on.

65
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:50,960
And people could block it, just by counting,

66
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:52,480
or by thinking about animals.

67
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:54,740
So you try it.

68
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:56,420
You think as hard as you can.

69
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:56,880
Penguin.

70
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:57,620
Giraffe.

71
00:01:57,960 --> 00:01:58,200
Hamster.

72
00:01:58,480 --> 00:01:58,660
Penguin.

73
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:00,400
The stranger winces.

74
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:01,100
Okay, okay.

75
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:01,360
Stop.

76
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:02,140
That's so annoying.

77
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:03,700
It works on them too.

78
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:06,720
Brain implants go even further.

79
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:09,760
In 2023, doctors in San Francisco placed a

80
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:11,540
thin sheet of sensors on the brain of

81
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:12,500
a woman named Ann.

82
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,800
A stroke had left her unable to speak

83
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:16,040
for eighteen years.

84
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,440
The sensors picked up the signals for the

85
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:19,580
sounds she was trying to make.

86
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:22,460
A computer turned them into words, spoken by

87
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:23,580
an avatar on a screen.

88
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:25,500
About seventy-eight words a minute.

89
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:28,080
Normal conversation is about a hundred and sixty.

90
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:30,020
But for Ann, it was a voice again.

91
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:32,740
And in 2024, the first person with a

92
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:35,060
Neuralink implant moved a cursor and played chess

93
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:37,100
on a laptop, just by thinking.

94
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:40,000
The stranger lowers their hood.

95
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:42,480
My name is Kai, they say.

96
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:44,860
And I'm not the only one.

97
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:48,360
Behind them, on the rooftops, four shapes are

98
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:48,740
watching.

99
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:51,400
We sent you the message after the lake,

100
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:52,200
Kai says.

101
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:54,260
We saw what you did under the ice.

102
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:55,340
In the gym.

103
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:56,500
At the crossing.

104
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:58,240
There are more of us.

105
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,140
And something's coming that we can't handle on

106
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:01,520
our own.

107
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:02,540
Tomorrow.

108
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:03,720
Six a.m.

109
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:05,820
The old observatory on the hill.

110
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:07,580
Then Kai smiles.

111
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:09,840
And stop thinking about penguins.

112
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:10,560
It's distracting.

113
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:14,020
So if you could read one person's mind,

114
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:14,880
whose would it be?

115
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:16,080
Tell us in the comments.

116
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:18,940
And subscribe, because next time, you meet the

117
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:19,340
others.

118
00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:21,900
Starting with the one who can fly.
